The literature on the Italian North-South divide is longstanding. It dates back to the 20th century and gave rise to a vivid debate, especially in the post-Second World War period, with the seminal works of Saraceno, Graziani and Rossi Doria, to name a few. Today, this stream of literature does not cease to receive contributions by academics (Felice, 2013;Viesti, 2013) and institutional research alike (e.g., see the Bank of Italy's Economic History Working Papers, and the series Rapporto SVIMEZ sull'Economia del Mezzogiorno edited yearly by SVIMEZ). While in a century and a half of Italian history regional disparities in economic development have involved regions of all longitude and latitude, the North-South gap persists and is still a major concern for both economists and policy-makers (Iuzzolino, Pellegrini, & Viesti, 2011). Almost all the contributions on internal skilled mobility in both the economic and sociodemographic literature adopt a North-South outlook. Thus, it seems appropriate to relate our findings also to the North-South divide debate. 6. Possible explanations to the prevailing of ante lauream male migration over female migration in Italy are explored in the relevant sociodemographic literature (Capuano, 2012;Impicciatore & Tuorto, 2011). 7. Structural equations are an approach to statistical modelling that allows one to address different kinds of problems including endogeneitythrough simultaneity of estimate (Hoyle, 2012;Wooldridge, 2002). Systems of structural equations also make it possible to specify direct and indirect paths between both observable and latent variables (Bollen, 1989). 8. Additional information on parents' occupational profile initially considered in the analysis was eventually excluded because of its very high correlation with parental education. Unfortunately, the data do not include any information on family income. 9. The score results from the average of different subscores representing five dimensions of educational quality:

(1) services offered by universities to enrolled students;

(2) scholarships and other interventions in support of the right to study; (3) buildings and infrastructures; (4) communication capacity and e-services; and (5) international reach. For the scores

